{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-75944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The matrix $D$ is given below. Let ${d}_{1}$, ${d}_{2}$, and ${d}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $D.$ Determine if $B=\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(D).$ If that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}$, where ${d}_{3}={x}_{1}{d}_{1}+{x}_{2}{d}_{2}.$\n\n$$\nD=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -7 & -17\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072104/state.json" "file_sha256" "feecadad2d10121d0f138d6fc3adc9843b6280859d98f77c1bfc1cd903d32fc0"} {"question" "q-75944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we reduce the matrix $D$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & -7 & -17\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -8 & -16\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=(-\\frac{1}{8}){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $2$nd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$ is ${{d}_{1},{d}_{2}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $3$rd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${d}_{3}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -17\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{3}}={{(3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{1}}+{{(2)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{2}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{d}_{2}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{2}=3\\cdot 2=6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072104/state.json" "file_sha256" "feecadad2d10121d0f138d6fc3adc9843b6280859d98f77c1bfc1cd903d32fc0"} {"question" "q-75944" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072104/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "bba728d73e286d9837877397aae3a7aace0726e78f07aa8edd9b1007daaab704"} {"question" "q-75944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072104/state.json" "file_sha256" "feecadad2d10121d0f138d6fc3adc9843b6280859d98f77c1bfc1cd903d32fc0"} {"question" "q-75944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "5"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "9"] ["text" "$\\text{{}{d}_{1},{d}_{2}\\text{}}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(D)$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072104/state.json" "file_sha256" "feecadad2d10121d0f138d6fc3adc9843b6280859d98f77c1bfc1cd903d32fc0"} {"question" "q-75944" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072104/state.json" "file_sha256" "feecadad2d10121d0f138d6fc3adc9843b6280859d98f77c1bfc1cd903d32fc0"} {"question" "q-75956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The matrix $W$ is given below. Let ${w}_{1}$, ${w}_{2}$, and ${w}_{3}$ be the respective columns of $W.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{w}_{1},{w}_{3}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(W).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}$, where ${w}_{2}={x}_{1}{w}_{1}+{x}_{3}{w}_{3}.$\n\n$$\nW=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072104/state.json" "file_sha256" "feecadad2d10121d0f138d6fc3adc9843b6280859d98f77c1bfc1cd903d32fc0"} {"question" "q-75956" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we reduce the matrix $W$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -6 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -12 & 9\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st and $3$rd columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(W)$ is ${{w}_{1},{w}_{3}},$ that is to say,\n\n$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the $2$nd column of the reduced matrix is a non-pivot column, then the entries in that column tell us the coefficients needed to express ${w}_{2}$ as a linear combination of all the other columns in the original matrix. So, we have\n\n$$\n{{[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ -12\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{2}}={{(-3)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{1}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{1}}+{{(0)}_{⏟}}_{{x}_{3}}\\cdot {{[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 9\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{w}_{3}}.\n$$\n\nThis gives us ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{3}=(-3)\\cdot 0=0.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14072104/state.json" "file_sha256" "feecadad2d10121d0f138d6fc3adc9843b6280859d98f77c1bfc